<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Myrrh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Myrrh is an aromatic resin obtained from trees native to north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, prized in antiquity for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, perfume and embalming. In the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> it was a key import from the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s expedition to Punt brought back myrrh trees along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and exotic animals.</p></div>
